[SIZE=6][B]BlocksAdder Documentation[/B][/SIZE]
[B]Author:[/B] Lino
[B]Development format:[/B] blocksadder/bundle-1
[B]Target servers:[/B] Spigot/Paper, Minecraft Java 1.21.11â€“26.3
[B]Java:[/B] 21 for 1.21.11; 25 for 26.x

[SIZE=5][B]Installation and version scope[/B][/SIZE]
Install the JAR matching this documentation in plugins/ and restart. Development 1.1.0-SNAPSHOT uses the bundle format below. Historical release 1.0.0 is a separate preview with an older format and a smaller feature set. Download only from [URL=https://github.com/lino9999/BlocksAdder/releases]GitHub Releases[/URL] and read the matching release notes.
Custom visuals need the generated resource pack. ProtocolLib is optional; specific camera/equipment-view and tile-NBT adapters have separate requirements. Folia and Bedrock packs are unsupported.

[SIZE=5][B]Content documents[/B][/SIZE]
Content belongs under plugins/BlocksAdder/contents/. Each complete file has a format marker, bundle identifier and typed record list:
[CODE]format: blocksadder/bundle-1
bundle: garden
entries:
  - key: sapphire
    kind: artifact
    item:
      base: AMETHYST_SHARD
      label: '&bSapphire'
      details: ['&7A bright crafting ingredient.']
      group: materials
      sprite: garden:item/sapphire
      stack: 64
    traits:
      unbreakable: false
  - key: chair
    kind: furnishing
    item:
      base: PAPER
      label: '&bGarden Chair'
      sprite: minecraft:item/amethyst_shard
    settings:
      width: 0.8
      height: 1.0
      seat: true
  - key: materials
    kind: catalog
    settings:
      title: Materials[/CODE]
The IDs above are garden:sapphire and garden:chair. Keep existing IDs stable after placing content. Set active: false on a record or bundle to disable it. External plugin configurations and flat namespace/item documents are rejected.

Presentation fields belong under item: base, label, details, group, visual, sprite, parent, variant and stack. Other item properties belong under traits. World settings belong under settings for block, furnishing or crop records. Each file's entries may mix definition kinds. Do not duplicate an item's presentation fields inside traits.

[SIZE=5][B]Features and record kinds[/B][/SIZE]
[LIST]
[*][B]artifact:[/B] custom inventory items with stable identity, model data, durability/usages, enchantments, attributes, colors, food, equipment and action plans.
[*][B]block:[/B] placeable blocks, timed mining/tool rules, protected removal, persistent identity, native carrier states, sounds and owned light.
[*][B]furnishing:[/B] display/frame/armor-stand carriers, hitboxes, transformations, seats and managed footprints. Advanced combinations remain under development.
[*][B]crop / crop-series:[/B] seeds, growth stages, maturity, bone meal, harvest rewards and protected natural destruction.
[*][B]recipe:[/B] shaped/shapeless crafting, furnace, blasting, smoking, campfire, stonecutting, smithing and anvil rules.
[*][B]catalog:[/B] item browser groups.
[*][B]glyph / audio:[/B] bitmap font images, permission-aware text aliases and custom sound definitions.
[*][B]meter / overlay:[/B] bounded persistent player statistics and HUDs.
[*][B]drop-rule / block-reward:[/B] conditional rewards for world, entity and fishing behavior.
[*][B]creature / rig / motion / vehicle:[/B] living entities, skeletal models, player disguises/emotes and controlled mounts.
[*][B]tree / ore-field / surface-cover / tree-field:[/B] manual trees and bounded automatic population.
[*][B]fluid / flame:[/B] custom liquid and fire definitions.
[*][B]armor-set / armor-effect:[/B] equipment assets and rendering settings; consult their current acceptance limits.
[/LIST]
This list describes development modules, not certification of every original option or visual effect. Read the [URL=https://github.com/lino9999/BlocksAdder/blob/main/docs/parity-progress.md]implementation tracker[/URL] and module guides before production use.

[SIZE=5][B]Recipe example[/B][/SIZE]
Add another record to entries:
[CODE]  - key: sapphire_block
    kind: recipe
    settings:
      type: shaped
      result: garden:sapphire_block
      shape: ['SS', 'SS']
      ingredients:
        S: garden:sapphire[/CODE]
The result must be defined separately. Custom recipe ingredients use persistent identity; vanilla lookalikes do not satisfy custom ingredients. Supported recipe-specific settings and limitations are in the recipe guide.

[SIZE=5][B]Resource-pack assets and hosting[/B][/SIZE]
Place the example texture at contents/garden/resourcepack/assets/garden/textures/item/sapphire.png. Supply models, textures, audio and fonts you own or may redistribute. Top-level resourcepack/assets/ overrides matching pack assets. YAML and private analysis files are excluded from the generated pack.
[CODE]resource-pack:
  build-on-startup: true
  auto-apply: false
  required: false
  url: 'https://assets.example.com/BlocksAdder.zip'
  host:
    enabled: false
    bind: '0.0.0.0'
    port: 8163[/CODE]
These server settings belong in config.yml. Run /ba pack build and upload the exact pack/BlocksAdder.zip to a direct HTTP(S) URL. Built-in hosting optionally serves only /BlocksAdder.zip through GET/HEAD; configure an available separate port and your public URL. DNS, TLS and router forwarding are configured separately. A failed build retains the last successful artifact.

[SIZE=5][B]Commands and permissions[/B][/SIZE]
[CODE]/ba help
/ba browse [category] [player]
/ba get <item> [amount]
/ba give <player> <item> [amount] [silent]
/ba recipe <item> [player]
/ba inspect
/ba plants <auto|full|hide|status> [player]
/ba validate
/ba reload
/ba pack <build|send|hash> [player|all][/CODE]
Main aliases: /blocksadder, /ba and /badd. The [URL=https://github.com/lino9999/BlocksAdder#commands-and-permissions]complete README table[/URL] covers administration, model/skin, emote, liquid/fire, vehicle, generation, HUD/stat, sound, glyph, durability/color and configuration commands with their individual permissions. blocksadder.admin grants administration; ordinary building uses blocksadder.place, blocksadder.break and blocksadder.use. Controlling another player's supported features requires blocksadder.others.
Configuration commands preview the selected edits, require repeating the same command, reject stale previews and restore previous bytes on activation failure where recovery succeeds.

[SIZE=5][B]Personal plant detail[/B][/SIZE]
Use /ba plants auto for distance-based crowd detail, full for normal models within the range/cap, hide to hide crops and their interaction helpers, and status to read the saved preference. blocksadder.plants is granted by default; targeting someone else requires blocksadder.others. Hidden plants continue natural/API growth, but native clicks cannot harvest or fertilize them.
Server settings under plants.view control radius, maximum, crowded-at, thin-after, card-after, field-of-view and shared per-tick work/time budgets. Crop settings may supply visuals.far-stages and visuals.facing for matching stage models that face the camera. The [URL=https://github.com/lino9999/BlocksAdder/blob/main/docs/crop-views.md]plant-view guide[/URL] includes a complete bundle example and generated-card asset settings. Visual checks belong to the user.

[SIZE=5][B]Verification and independent implementation[/B][/SIZE]
[B]Held interactions:[/B] Native managed inputs run held item plans after target protection and before physical plans. Both hands retain their proper trigger; offhand use does not replay seating or harvesting. Model helpers resolve their owning placement through cached references. Held cancellation stops the built-in operation, and placement/bone-meal/shortcut consumption preserves unrelated callback replacements. The [URL=https://github.com/lino9999/BlocksAdder/blob/main/docs/held-world-interactions.md]held interaction guide[/URL] includes the complete bundle example, trigger mapping and exact scope. API furniture calls without native input retain physical-only preparation.

[B]Placed-content plans:[/B] Item-bearing bundle records may define traits.events.world.use, world.remove and world.harvest. These run against the placed definition separately from the held item. Use and removal are preparation events: cancellation prevents the built-in operation, while already performed effects cannot be rolled back. Inventory amount/durability/usage/model mutations are rejected for this read-only source. Delayed actions retain the exact placement; committed removal may retain an empty anchor until a new managed placement invalidates it. See the [URL=https://github.com/lino9999/BlocksAdder/blob/main/docs/world-plans.md]complete world-plan guide[/URL] for a current-format example and scope.

[B]Visual checks are performed by the user.[/B] Headless tests validate server behavior and generated data, while protocol scenarios exercise specific native player actions. They do not establish full functional parity or every rendering combination. Read [URL=https://github.com/lino9999/BlocksAdder/blob/main/docs/development-verification.md]development verification[/URL] for exact artifact/version scopes.
The [URL=https://github.com/lino9999/BlocksAdder/blob/main/docs/independent-implementation.md]distribution audit[/URL] compares built archive members, class paths, reference branding, commands, permissions and longer message literals. The implementation and configuration are independently designed. The audit does not guarantee a marketplace review outcome.

[B]Environmental removal:[/B] Crop and block bundle records may define traits.environment. Crops support water, lava, trampling, dry farmland, pistons, lost/burning support and explosions; custom blocks support explosions. Each rule can prevent removal or run an ordered list of cue, sparks, reward, experience, shockwave and console effects after saved removal. Reward and XP effects honor drop suppression. Delays retain the removed source and backing state; replacement, actual chunk unload or reload invalidates the applicable jobs. No player is invented for a natural event. See the [URL=https://github.com/lino9999/BlocksAdder/blob/main/docs/environment-removal.md]environment rule guide[/URL] for limits, cancellation and a complete independent-format example.

[B]Conditional world changes:[/B] The independent world_change action accepts match and into choices in ordered item/physical plans. It supports native/custom blocks in either direction, partial native states, explicit air and furnishing-to-furnishing changes. Preparation honors protection and exact source identity. Failed changes restore the prior state; ordinary native container contents and stored furnace XP are released only after committed type changes. Crop choices and occupied entity-store type migration remain separate work. See the [URL=https://github.com/lino9999/BlocksAdder/blob/main/docs/world-changes.md]world-change guide[/URL] for schema, limits and API.

[SIZE=5][B]Support[/B][/SIZE]
Report reproducible problems through [URL=https://github.com/lino9999/BlocksAdder/issues]GitHub Issues[/URL]. Include the BlocksAdder version, exact server build, Java version, relevant bundle records and logs. Back up saved worlds and configuration before updating.

[B]Area mining:[/B] Immediate held block_break plans accept world_sweep with a bounded region, optional horizontal facing frame and native/custom block selectors. Work starts after the origin commits, honors individual protection and tool rules, retains native drops/XP and stops on source replacement, exhaustion, permission loss or reload. A shared 32-cell-per-tick budget and exact placement snapshots bound work. See the [URL=https://github.com/lino9999/BlocksAdder/blob/main/docs/area-mining.md]area-mining guide[/URL].

[B]Projectile actions:[/B] Use traits.events.flight.launch, flight.block and flight.entity on item-bearing bundle records. Native arrows, tridents and throwable item carriers keep their own item identity, separately from held inventory. Impact receipts support delayed effects after native removal. Explicit damage attribution preserves native protection and killer credit without invoking unrelated held melee plans; particle origins follow the event. See the [URL=https://github.com/lino9999/BlocksAdder/blob/main/docs/projectile-actions.md]projectile guide[/URL] for schema, cancellation limits and lifecycle rules.

[SIZE=5][B]Typed particle actions[/B][/SIZE]
Inside an artifact's traits.events plan, use play_particle for a cloud or shoot_particle for a line. The particle name must exist on the installed server. Typed payloads include shade/scale for dust, state for native block data, item for vanilla/custom item particles and destination settings for trails/vibrations. Colors must be quoted hexadecimal strings.
[CODE]
play_particle:
  name: DUST
  payload:
    shade: '#22b8cf'
    scale: 1.25
  emit:
    count: 12
    spread: [0.2, 0.2, 0.2]
    speed: 0
[/CODE]
A line accepts integer distance 1–32 and samples every half block. Optional emit settings are count, spread, speed, offset and force, with a 4,096-particle action budget. Count zero enables the native directional mode. Unloaded samples/destinations are skipped without loading their chunks. Trails and vibrations can target emitter, player or victim; an unshifted player/victim vibration can follow its native entity. Newer geyser particles require a server that provides them.
[URL=https://github.com/lino9999/BlocksAdder/blob/main/docs/particle-actions.md]Full payload table, destination rules and verification scope[/URL]

[SIZE=5][B]Native effects and custom totems[/B][/SIZE]
play_effect accepts a strict payload selected by the server's public effect API. Use block for block materials, face for one of six native directions, shade for quoted hexadecimal RGB, amount for bone meal, axis for X/Y/Z, and the documented native value/boolean fields for other families. Optional emit.radius is 1–256 (default 64); emit.offset is a finite three-number world-axis vector within ±64. Unloaded chunks are skipped.
[CODE]
play_effect:
  name: SMOKE
  payload:
    face: UP
  emit:
    radius: 48
[/CODE]
RECORD_PLAY accepts record: minecraft:music_disc_cat or a registered native song key; record: minecraft:air stops playback. Obsolete door/trapdoor/gate effects and Spigot's removed event 2005 are rejected. Dragon breath uses an explicit audible boolean so its sound control has the same meaning across forks.
[CODE]
play_totem_animation:
  item: symbols:crest
[/CODE]
The display uses a copy of the configured item, retains its material/model/component data and restores the current client hand in a finally block. Server inventory/counts and health remain unchanged. Source changes, reloads and disconnects cancel stale delayed item plans. An unsupported or lossy public component round trip rejects the display before use.
Use [B]/ba totem <player> [item] [--quiet][/B] with permission [B]blocksadder.totem[/B]. The optional item is a custom namespaced ID; --quiet hides the success confirmation. Omit it for the vanilla animation.
[URL=https://github.com/lino9999/BlocksAdder/blob/main/docs/effects-and-totems.md]Full payload table, native compatibility notes, API and verification boundaries[/URL]

[B]Development preview 13 camera compatibility:[/B] fixes the tested Paper 26.3/ProtocolLib movement disconnect. Native camera input and lifecycle pass on nine builds; exact server/protocol scope and remaining visual checks are recorded in the [URL=https://github.com/lino9999/BlocksAdder/blob/main/docs/camera-input.md]camera verification guide[/URL].

[B]Image HUDs and player meters:[/B] Development preview 14 adds full/partial/empty icon bars, quantity frames, custom API-controlled icon sequences, named fonts and pixel positioning. Shared meters retain independent bounds and source-specific bypass, with eligible player timers, world/biome/light rules, food/respawn changes and protected depleted effects. Read the [URL=https://github.com/lino9999/BlocksAdder/blob/main/docs/hud-meters.md]HUD and meter guide[/URL] for the independent bundle schema and exact limits.

[SIZE=5][B]PlaceholderAPI exports[/B][/SIZE]
Install an official compatible PlaceholderAPI build separately and restart the server. BlocksAdder registers its internal blocksadder expansion without an eCloud download. In config.yml:
[CODE]integrations:
  placeholders: true[/CODE]
Run /ba reload after changing this optional setting. False detaches the expansion while core features continue. Build and distribute the resource pack while the adapter is active to include scalar spacing support.

These examples assume registered garden:heart glyph and garden:stamina meter definitions:
[LIST]
[*][B]%blocksadder_glyph_garden:heart%[/B]: glyph character followed by minus-one-pixel spacing.
[*][B]%blocksadder_code_garden:heart%[/B]: raw character without spacing; this is not a numeric Unicode code point.
[*][B]%blocksadder_font_garden:heart%[/B]: configured font identifier, defaulting to minecraft:default.
[*][B]%blocksadder_move_-8%[/B]: signed pixel movement. Integers from -1048576 through 1048576 are accepted; zero returns an empty string.
[*][B]%blocksadder_meter_garden:stamina%[/B]: current bounded online player value, such as 17.5 or 20.0.
[/LIST]
Canonical namespaced IDs are recommended. Local shorthand works only when unique within its glyph or meter catalog; underscores, dots and slashes inside keys are preserved. Ambiguous or malformed requests and unknown glyphs remain unresolved. A syntactically valid unknown meter or unavailable player produces 0.0. A previously unknown OfflinePlayer wrapper first requested asynchronously also receives 0.0 while bounded main-thread resolution is queued; later requests can read the resolved online snapshot. These exports never mutate meters or execute actions.

PlaceholderAPI strings cannot carry font metadata. Configure the consumer's font explicitly, using the font export where supported. Active-adapter pack builds place the generated blocksadder:layout reference before existing providers in the default font and available registered glyph fonts, preserving authored properties and available overlays. Missing external font files are not invented. The layout font and characters U+F1000 through U+F1029 are reserved; explicit in-pack conflicts are rejected, while external font/TTF/unihex coverage cannot be exhaustively checked. Keep that range free when merging packs.

Glyph exports are trusted server-template data and do not enforce chat glyph permissions. Ordinary chat/sign/book aliases retain their permission checks. Restrict template editing and apply viewer conditions in the consuming plugin when required.

The expansion persists through /papi reload and preserves foreign expansion ownership. Plain plugin-manager disable/enable can leave official PlaceholderAPI 2.12.3 without its initialized singleton; BlocksAdder then reports the failed adapter connection while core features remain available. Restart the server for provider lifecycle changes. A private fixture's controlled public onLoad replay is not generic hot-reload support. Rendered appearance remains the user's check; this reference does not claim a completed native compatibility matrix.
[URL=https://github.com/lino9999/BlocksAdder/blob/main/docs/placeholders.md]Full placeholder syntax, threading, font merging and lifecycle reference[/URL]

[B]PlaceholderAPI command templates[/B]
Add placeholder_api: true to an administrator-configured execute_commands entry to resolve external values in its arguments. The default is false. The command label must remain a literal; replacements accept only 1-1024 ASCII letters, digits, underscore, dot, colon, plus or minus. Templates/results are limited to 8192 characters and 32 external tokens. Spaces, formatting, slashes, free-text output and recursive expansion are unsupported.

Example command: ba stat write {player} garden:mirror %blocksadder_meter_garden:stamina%
Use as_console: true and silent: true for this registered plugin command. See the complete bundle example in the [URL=https://github.com/lino9999/BlocksAdder/blob/main/docs/command-placeholders.md]command-placeholder guide[/URL].

Resolution uses the acting player on the main thread. Missing/uninitialized providers, unknown or unsafe output and invalidated source/inventory/permission/registry/provider state fail the action without dispatch. Delays resolve at execution time. Incoming support is independent of the outgoing export setting/prefix; the example above itself requires the BlocksAdder meter export. Trust configured commands and installed expansion plugins: scalar validation does not prove semantic safety for privileged templates or roll back external callback side effects.

[B]Surface regions and additional rewards[/B]
Configure an immediate block_break world_sweep with region min/max triples and frame: surface for the actual clicked face. Alternatively use region: {column: 4}; columns accept horizontal clicked faces and range from 2 to 343 cells including the origin. preserve: [resources, mechanisms] retains registered custom blocks, native resources and supported native mechanisms. rewards accepts mode: natural, none or origin, plus silk-required: true or false. Suppressed rewards still allow protected removals and ordinary wear.

Native reward relocation emits cancellable AreaRewardMoveEvent. Configured/custom loot and supported Container escrow choose their destination before spawning. Native experience stays at each extra cell; furnace recipe-XP caches and non-Container storage payloads are not claimed. The shared budget is 32 attempted cells per tick; missing chunks are never loaded. See the [URL=https://github.com/lino9999/BlocksAdder/blob/main/docs/area-mining.md]complete schema, event and storage contract[/URL].
